                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "In a vacuum, Toby: The Secret Mine is a short and serviceable platformer that has some issues, in comparison to the obvious inspiration it loses out. Still, I can recommend it, but with a huge grain of salt.\n\nStorywise there's not much going on, there's evil guys with red eyes that captured your white-eyed friends and you try to stop them. There's multiple [spoiler]two[/spoiler] endings, so there's a bit of replayability, which is a plus point in my books.\n\nFor the gameplay, you can run, jump and sometimes intereact with stuff, like levers, puzzles etc. Controls feel okay, but not outstanding, the hitboxes for some interactables are almost too strict for my liking. The level design is largely alright, but there's quite a few sections where it's hard to see where you're going. What I'm not too much a fan of in this kind of platformer is that at some places you **will** die on your first playthrough as you run into traps you can't see. As a plus point to that though, checkpoints are plenty and a death never sets you back too much. Another thing I wasn't quite happy with is that some puzzles don't seem to make too much sense, unless I somehow missed hints for them before. Overall though, the difficulty is not too high, everyone with a bit of platformer experience should be able to beat the game in a few hours.\n\nGraphically there is not much to say, it's clearly inspired by Limbo and looks the part. Sound is largely fitting, though the music that played whenever one of the evil guys showed up got old after the third time.\n\nFor the settings, it's again alright but not outstanding. You have the classic unity setup window before the game launches, in which you can set the resolution, the graphics quality (which tbh I've never seen having any impact in any unity game I've played, so no idea if it does something here) and the option to rebind keys. I played with a contoller, didn't have to set up anything, worked out of the box. Ingame, there are no settings, so you'll have to tab out and modify the volume in your OS's audio mixer.\n\nAchievements are all obtainable, one is for missable collectables, the game offers a level selection so it's easy to go back if you missed some.\n\nSo up until here, it's an alright platformer, maybe a bit weak on the settings site, but it comes comes with a huge weakness at the end: Being heavily inspired by Limbo makes it impossible not to compare the two, and it loses that comparison. The story of Limbo has impact, while it's kinda just there in Toby. The overall atmosphere in Limbo is more of a complete package compared to Toby, same with the sound design. It's almost as if the dev really liked Limbo, but either not fully grasped what makes it great, or wasn't quite able to reproduce that.\n\nSo for the hard part, how do I rate this? If Steam gave me the option for a neutral review, I'd chose it, no question. But since I have to decide, I'll give it a thumbs up with a huge asterisk. There's quite a few aspects I didn't quite like, and for what it is, the non-sale price is a really big ask. But still, losing the comparison to one of the best indie puzzle platformers out there doesn't make it bad, technically it runs well, while it does have issues it still can be enjoyable overall, and it goes on deep sale pretty often. So yeah, I think the current score of 65% positive is also how I'd rate it.",
